WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:06.392
A bowl. A ball. Watch it step down on my
count. One. Two, three.

00:00:06.551 --> 00:00:11.210
I wrote the words and drew the pictures,
and each picture waits for its word.

00:00:12.214 --> 00:00:14.248
You just watched DeckTalk.

00:00:15.291 --> 00:00:17.592
You write a script and simple slides.

00:00:17.961 --> 00:00:22.082
It reads your script aloud,
and you pick the word each picture waits

00:00:22.082 --> 00:00:25.847
for. Change the script,
and the video follows.

00:00:26.297 --> 00:00:28.145
Here is a lesson I made with it.

00:00:28.746 --> 00:00:31.454
Then I'll change one sentence.

00:00:32.032 --> 00:00:35.506
How does AI learn? From examples,
not rules people write.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:37.979
An AI model is like a box of knobs.

00:00:38.011 --> 00:00:42.077
It guesses, and a meter shows its error,
how wrong the guess was.

00:00:42.458 --> 00:00:46.954
Learning turns the knobs until the meter
drops, example after example.

00:00:47.903 --> 00:00:50.019
But which way should each knob turn?

00:00:50.214 --> 00:00:54.474
In a big model, testing knobs one at a
time would take billions of tries for

00:00:54.474 --> 00:00:59.760
every step. Instead, one round of math
gives a direction for every knob at once.

00:01:00.662 --> 00:01:06.308
Picture two knobs. The floor is every way
to set them. The height is the error.

00:01:06.978 --> 00:01:09.094
For this lesson it is a bowl.

00:01:10.984 --> 00:01:16.000
The ball is where the knobs are set right
now. It can't see the whole bowl,

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.930
only the slope where it stands.

00:01:18.113 --> 00:01:22.281
So it steps downhill, again and again,
and the steps shrink as the ground

00:01:22.281 --> 00:01:25.430
flattens. That is gradient descent.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:27.728
It's what the ball did on my count.

00:01:28.573 --> 00:01:33.600
One setting makes every step longer or
shorter. Too long, and it bounces past the

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.923
bottom. Too short, and it is very slow.

00:01:38.442 --> 00:01:43.053
Zoom out, and the ground can have many
valleys, each about as low.

00:01:45.698 --> 00:01:51.073
Today's AI is trained with a version of
this. One step takes billions of billions

00:01:51.073 --> 00:01:55.276
of small sums. Inside,
the knobs are grids of numbers,

00:01:55.276 --> 00:02:00.259
called tensors. Chips like GPUs do math on
a whole grid at once.

00:02:01.127 --> 00:02:04.555
Big models split the work across thousands
of chips.

00:02:05.281 --> 00:02:09.809
That's the lesson. Now I'll change one
sentence, the kind of edit that used to

00:02:09.809 --> 00:02:11.205
mean recording again.

00:02:11.608 --> 00:02:14.502
Say a viewer asks why downhill is good.

00:02:14.766 --> 00:02:20.839
So I add four words, and make the video
again. Nothing re-recorded or re-edited by

00:02:20.839 --> 00:02:26.284
hand. The height is the error,
so lower is better. Every later picture

00:02:26.284 --> 00:02:28.190
moved to stay on its word.

00:02:28.968 --> 00:02:33.542
Update your video the way you update a
doc. DeckTalk. Open source,

00:02:33.542 --> 00:02:35.832
narrated presentations, cut to the word.

00:02:35.992 --> 00:02:38.317
Make your own at decktalk dot app.
